00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.790
GABE WEISS: With almost
500 sessions at Next 2019,

00:00:02.790 --> 00:00:05.072
it's understandable that you
couldn't get to them all.

00:00:05.072 --> 00:00:07.530
So if you didn't see Worried
about application performance?

00:00:07.530 --> 00:00:08.400
Cache it!

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:10.230
Then stay tuned, because
here's the Rewind.

00:00:10.230 --> 00:00:12.226
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:15.220 --> 00:00:16.810
When developing an
application, there

00:00:16.810 --> 00:00:18.850
are several things
that are fundamental--

00:00:18.850 --> 00:00:22.360
speed, response time,
availability, and cost to run.

00:00:22.360 --> 00:00:24.340
It doesn't matter how
good your application is.

00:00:24.340 --> 00:00:26.330
If it stutters or takes
too long to respond,

00:00:26.330 --> 00:00:28.150
it'll never succeed.

00:00:28.150 --> 00:00:29.920
In-memory caching
is a common pattern

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:32.710
you see to achieve that
performance that you need.

00:00:32.710 --> 00:00:34.870
In-memory caching helps
reduce latency and cost

00:00:34.870 --> 00:00:37.203
by taking frequently
accessed data

00:00:37.203 --> 00:00:39.370
and retrieving it from
memory, when possible, rather

00:00:39.370 --> 00:00:40.340
than disk.

00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:42.340
There's quite a bit going
on beneath the surface

00:00:42.340 --> 00:00:44.150
to properly do caching.

00:00:44.150 --> 00:00:46.410
So this means that while
it's a common concept,

00:00:46.410 --> 00:00:48.910
there's plenty to learn about
optimization and your options,

00:00:48.910 --> 00:00:51.960
particularly around
using a Redis process.

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:55.270
Redis is an open source
in-memory data structure store.

00:00:55.270 --> 00:00:58.090
That means it holds anything
you put into it in active memory

00:00:58.090 --> 00:00:59.570
instead of on disk.

00:00:59.570 --> 00:01:02.080
Redis is popular because it's
more than just a key value

00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:02.718
store.

00:01:02.718 --> 00:01:04.760
It supports a bunch of
different data structures.

00:01:04.760 --> 00:01:06.920
It has high availability
and persistence.

00:01:06.920 --> 00:01:09.140
Redis is usable for many
different use cases,

00:01:09.140 --> 00:01:10.987
not just for caching.

00:01:10.987 --> 00:01:12.820
When you're moving to
the cloud, getting rid

00:01:12.820 --> 00:01:15.280
of infrastructure management
is often top of the mind.

00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:18.010
And that includes managing
and monitoring your cache.

00:01:18.010 --> 00:01:21.100
So that's where Google's
Cloud Memorystore comes in.

00:01:21.100 --> 00:01:22.750
Memorystore's a
managed solution,

00:01:22.750 --> 00:01:24.980
fully compatible with
the Redis protocol,

00:01:24.980 --> 00:01:26.860
which means you get all
the awesome of Redis

00:01:26.860 --> 00:01:28.600
without any of the management.

00:01:28.600 --> 00:01:30.760
Memorystore helps you
optimize in three main ways

00:01:30.760 --> 00:01:32.140
since it's a managed service.

00:01:32.140 --> 00:01:35.650
One, updates and security
patching are all taken care of.

00:01:35.650 --> 00:01:38.170
Two, it's got high
availability and reliability

00:01:38.170 --> 00:01:40.150
with automatic failover
and replication.

00:01:40.150 --> 00:01:43.060
And three, it's scalable
and it's secure.

00:01:43.060 --> 00:01:45.550
Cloud Memorystore comes
in two different packages

00:01:45.550 --> 00:01:46.900
to suit your needs.

00:01:46.900 --> 00:01:49.540
First, the basic tier-- it's
a standalone regional instance

00:01:49.540 --> 00:01:50.380
of Redis.

00:01:50.380 --> 00:01:54.440
And then the standard tier is a
cross-zone replicated instance.

00:01:54.440 --> 00:01:57.250
Since the GA launch six
months ago, many updates

00:01:57.250 --> 00:02:00.280
have been added to Memorystore,
including manual failover

00:02:00.280 --> 00:02:03.130
for testing, access from
App Engine second generation

00:02:03.130 --> 00:02:06.280
runtimes, more regions,
metrics, and some compliance

00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:07.480
certifications.

00:02:07.480 --> 00:02:09.650
But these expansions
don't stop there.

00:02:09.650 --> 00:02:12.100
The team's currently working
to add many more capabilities

00:02:12.100 --> 00:02:14.830
to Memorystore--
things like shared VPC

00:02:14.830 --> 00:02:16.960
and instance-level
authentication and Redis

00:02:16.960 --> 00:02:18.340
clustering.

00:02:18.340 --> 00:02:19.810
In addition to a
live demo showing

00:02:19.810 --> 00:02:21.670
how simple it is
to set all this up,

00:02:21.670 --> 00:02:23.620
the talk goes over
some best practices

00:02:23.620 --> 00:02:26.140
for storing data, eviction
policies, and memory

00:02:26.140 --> 00:02:28.120
management, and then
a breakdown of what

00:02:28.120 --> 00:02:29.770
to monitor from
Stackdriver-- things

00:02:29.770 --> 00:02:33.820
like CPU usage and alerts, keys
present in memory, failovers,

00:02:33.820 --> 00:02:35.902
and how to best scale
based on growth.

